><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Saladin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Saladin (Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub) was a Kurdish Muslim military leader and the founder of the Ayyubid Empire, who died in 1193.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salaga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salaga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salaga</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salaga is a town in the Savannah Region of northern Ghana, in the kingdom of Gonja.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salagama"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salagama.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salagama</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Salagama are a Sinhalese caste of the south-western coast of Sri Lanka, traditionally associated with weaving and later with peeling cinnamon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salah-bey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salah-bey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salah Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salah Bey was the Ottoman bey of Constantine, in eastern Algeria, from 1771 to 1792, under the authority of the dey of Algiers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salah-rais"><span class="enc-name">Salah Rais</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salah Rais (c. 1488–1556) was an Ottoman corsair and admiral who served as beylerbey of the Regency of Algiers from 1552 to 1556.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salaman-and-absal"><span class="enc-name">Salaman and Absal</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Salaman and Absal is an allegorical poem in Persian by Jami, one of the seven narrative poems of his Haft Awrang, dedicated to the Aq Qoyunlu sultan Ya'qub.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salamanca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salamanca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salamanca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salamanca is a city in Castile and León, Spain, on the River Tormes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salamis-island"><span class="enc-name">Salamis Island</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salamis is the largest Greek island in the Saronic Gulf, lying close to the coast of Attica west of Piraeus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salamis, Cyprus</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salamis was an ancient city on the east coast of Cyprus, north of modern Famagusta, founded in the 11th century BCE as successor to Enkomi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salamiyah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salamiyah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salamiyah</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salamiyah is a town in central Syria, south-east of Hama on the edge of the steppe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salammbo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salammbo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salammbô</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Salammbô is a historical novel by the French writer Gustave Flaubert, published in 1862.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salapia"><span class="enc-name">Salapia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salapia was an ancient Daunian town on the Adriatic coast of northern Apulia, near the lagoon later called the Salpi lake.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salar-jung-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salar Jung I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salar Jung I, born Mir Turab Ali Khan, was chief minister of Hyderabad State from 1853 until his death in 1883.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salar-jung-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salar Jung III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salar Jung III, born Mir Yousuf Ali Khan, was a Hyderabad noble, grandson of Salar Jung I, who served briefly as prime minister of the state in 1912–1914.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-museum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salar-jung-museum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salar Jung Museum</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Salar Jung Museum in Hyderabad houses the collection of Salar Jung III, including paintings, sculpture, manuscripts, arms, textiles and decorative arts from India…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salastambha"><span class="enc-name">Salastambha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salastambha was the founder of the Mlechchha dynasty of Kamarupa, which took power in Assam in the mid-7th century after the end of the Varman line of Bhaskaravarman.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salchak-toka"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salchak-toka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salchak Toka</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salchak Toka was the leader of the Tuvan People's Revolutionary Party from 1932.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/saldana"><span class="enc-name">Saldaña</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Saldaña is a town on the Carrión river in the north of the province of Palencia, Castile and León.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salduie"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salduie.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salduie</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salduie was an Iberian town of the Sedetani on the Ebro in north-eastern Spain, on the site of modern Zaragoza.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sale"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/sale.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salé</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salé is a city on the Atlantic coast of Morocco, on the north bank of the Bou Regreg estuary opposite Rabat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salehuddin-of-selangor"><span class="enc-name">Salehuddin of Selangor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salehuddin of Selangor, born Raja Lumu, was the first Sultan of Selangor, recognised around 1766 with the sanction of the ruler of Perak.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salem-witch-trials"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salem-witch-trials.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salem witch trials</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Salem witch trials were a series of prosecutions for witchcraft in the Province of Massachusetts Bay in 1692 and 1693, centred on Salem Village and the town of Salem.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salem-baden-wurttemberg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salem-baden-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salem, Baden-Württemberg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salem is a municipality in the Lake Constance district of Baden-Württemberg, Germany, a few kilometres north of the lake.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salem-massachusetts"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salem-massachusetts.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salem, Massachusetts</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salem is a coastal city in Essex County, Massachusetts, north-east of Boston.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salem-new-jersey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salem-new-jersey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salem, New Jersey</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salem is a city in south-western New Jersey near the Delaware River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salento"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salento.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salento</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salento is the peninsula at the south-eastern tip of Italy, the 'heel' of the Italian boot, in Apulia, with Lecce as its main city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salghurids"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salghurids.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salghurids</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Salghurids were a Turkmen dynasty of atabegs who ruled Fars in southern Iran from Shiraz between 1148 and 1282.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sali-berisha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/sali-berisha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sali Berisha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sali Berisha is an Albanian politician, a cardiologist who became leader of the Democratic Party in 1991.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks"><span class="enc-name">Salian Franks</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Salian Franks were the Frankish groups of the north-west, first recorded in Toxandria, in the present-day southern Netherlands, in the 4th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salic-law"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salic-law.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salic law</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Salic law, or Pactus Legis Salicae, is the law code of the Salian Franks, probably first issued under Clovis I in the early 6th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-mansur"><span class="enc-name">Salih ibn Mansur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salih ibn Mansur was an Arab of Himyarite descent who founded the Emirate of Nekor on the Rif coast of northern Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-mirdas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-mirdas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salih ibn Mirdas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salih ibn Mirdas was an emir of the Banu Kilab Arabs who founded the Mirdasid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-tarif"><span class="enc-name">Salih ibn Tarif</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salih ibn Tarif was the second leader of the Barghawata, a Berber confederation on the Atlantic coast of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salihiyya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salihiyya</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Salihiyya is a Sufi order founded in Mecca in the late 19th century by Muhammad Salih, a disciple in the Ahmadiyya tradition of Ahmad ibn Idris.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salim-al-atrash"><span class="enc-name">Salim al-Atrash</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salim al-Atrash was a Druze leader from the powerful al-Atrash family of the Hauran in southern Syria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salim-bin-abdallah"><span class="enc-name">Salim bin Abdallah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salim bin Abdallah was a Swahili-Arab trader from the East African coast, linked with Zanzibar, who settled at Nkhotakota on the western shore of Lake Malawi around 1845.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salim-ibn-rashid-al-kharusi"><span class="enc-name">Salim ibn Rashid al-Kharusi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salim ibn Rashid al-Kharusi was an Ibadi imam of Oman, elected by scholars and tribal leaders at Tanuf in 1913 in opposition to the sultan of Muscat and his British ties.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salim-rubaya-ali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salim-rubaya-ali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salim Rubaya Ali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salim Rubaya Ali was a Yemeni revolutionary who headed South Yemen's Presidential Council from 1969 to 1978 after the Corrective Move.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salima-machamba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salima-machamba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salima Machamba</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salima Machamba was the last queen of Mohéli in the Comoros, a descendant of the Merina royal family of Madagascar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salinar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salinar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salinar</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Salinar was a culture of the north coast of Peru, flourishing from about 400 to 200 BCE in the Chicama and Moche valleys.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salinguerra-ii-torelli"><span class="enc-name">Salinguerra II Torelli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salinguerra II Torelli was the leader of the Ghibelline Torelli family in Ferrara and for decades the city's dominant figure.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salinisation"><span class="enc-name">Salinisation</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Salinisation is the build-up of salts in soil, often caused by long-term irrigation in hot, dry climates where evaporation leaves mineral deposits behind.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salins-les-bains"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salins-les-bains.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salins-les-Bains</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salins-les-Bains is a town in the Jura in eastern France whose salt springs were exploited from the Middle Ages and made it one of the richest places of the Free County…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sallust"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/sallust.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sallust</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sallust (Gaius Sallustius Crispus) was a Roman politician and historian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salmas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salmas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salmas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salmas is a city in West Azerbaijan province in north-western Iran, on a plain north-west of Lake Urmia near the Turkish border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salò</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salò is a town on the western shore of Lake Garda in Lombardy, northern Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salodurum"><span class="enc-name">Salodurum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Salodurum was a Roman settlement on the River Aare in the province of Germania Superior, the origin of the Swiss town of Solothurn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salome-daughter-of-herodias"><span class="enc-name">Salome (daughter of Herodias)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salome was a Herodian princess, daughter of Herodias and Herod Philip, named by Josephus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salomon-gessner"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salomon-gessner.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Salomon Gessner</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salomon Gessner was a Swiss poet, painter and engraver from Zürich.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salomon-of-brittany"><span class="enc-name">Salomon of Brittany</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Salomon was king of Brittany from 857 to 874.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/saloninus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/saloninus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Saloninus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Saloninus was the younger son of the Roman emperor Gallienus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/salote-tupou-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/salote-tupou-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sālote Tupou III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sālote Tupou III was queen of Tonga from 1918 to 1965.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/salt-and-iron-monopoly"><span class="enc-name">Salt and iron monopoly</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The salt and iron monopoly was a state monopoly over the production and sale of salt and iron established under Emperor Wu of the Han Dynasty in the second century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-salt-ibn-malik"><span class="enc-name">Al-Salt ibn Malik</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Salt ibn Malik was Ibadi imam of Oman from 851 to 886, one of the longest-reigning rulers of the first imamate.</span></a></li></ul>
